Now let me write the design plan and then the plan entry to log.jsonl.

For a power tool battery:
- Application: power tools (drills, saws, grinders) — high discharge rate, fast charging, moderate energy density
- Nominal capacity ≥ 2 Ah — achievable with appropriate electrode dimensions
- 5C discharge retention ≥ 95% — this is quite demanding. At 5C, many NMC/graphite cells show ~80-90% retention. To get 95%, we need:
  - High electrolyte conductivity
  - Low resistance (thin electrodes, good transport)
  - Possibly higher transference number
  - Small particle size (short diffusion path)
- 4C fast charge without lithium plating — anode potential ≥ 0V at all times. This requires good electrolyte transport and appropriate N/P ratio
- T_max ≤ 60°C — need good thermal management (higher cooling h)
- Power density ≥ 4000 W/kg — this is achievable with Chen2020 type cells

The main challenges are balancing the 5C retention requirement against the thermal constraints, since high current rates generate more heat that needs to be dissipated.

For the first round, I'll start with the Chen2020 baseline parameters and test at both 1C and 5C to establish how the system performs natively. Then I'll adjust the electrode architecture — tweaking thickness, porosity, and particle size — while also tuning the thermal management and electrolyte properties like conductivity and transference number. In the second round, if the baseline doesn't meet the 95% retention target, I'll push harder on the electrolyte transport improvements, refine the thermal model, and consider more aggressive architecture changes like thinner electrodes with higher porosity.

Writing the design plan and log entry now...
</thinking>
